## Migration Analysis

Your code has three patterns that break on .NET 8:

| Pattern | Problem on .NET 8 |
|---------|-------------------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006) |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Obsolete (SYSLIB0046 in .NET 9), unreliable on `WaitOne` |
| `ManualResetEvent` + manual queue | Race condition: multiple `Enqueue` calls with one `Reset` can lose signals |

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _workerTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken token)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(token))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Clean shutdown requested
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();           // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard deadline

        if (_workerTask is not null)
            await _workerTask;
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` | `Channel<string>` | Thread-safe, backpressure-aware, no manual synchronization |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `_channel.Writer.Complete()` | Gracefully ends the reader loop |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter` | Cooperative hard deadline if drain takes too long |
| `new Thread(RunLoop)` | `Task.Run(...)` | Uses thread pool; `async` enables cancellation-aware waits |
| Busy `while(true)` + `WaitOne` | `await foreach` + `ReadAllAsync` | Zero-allocation wait; wakes only when items arrive or token fires |

## Pitfalls to Watch For

1. **Signal loss bug (existing):** Your original code had a race — two rapid `Enqueue` calls could result in only one `Set`/`Reset` cycle, losing an item. `Channel<T>` eliminates this entirely.

2. **Don't catch and swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — only catch it at the top-level loop. Let it propagate elsewhere.

3. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — it leaks timers if not disposed. The `IAsyncDisposable` pattern above handles this.

4. **If `Handle` is long-running**, pass the token into it so cancellation is responsive:
   ```csharp
   private async Task HandleAsync(string item, CancellationToken token) { ... }
   ```

5. **If you need synchronous-only** (no async allowed), use `BlockingCollection<string>` instead:
   ```csharp
   private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
   // In loop: _queue.Take(_cts.Token)
   // To stop: _queue.CompleteAdding(); _cts.Cancel();
   ```
